  "scope": [
    {
      "id": "COL-0190",
      "statement": "An actual integral finite-product tensor decomposition induces a decomposition of the specified fraction-field tensor product, with integral and fraction coordinates and map uniqueness.",
      "conditions": "Algebraic faithful domain algebras and a finite index type, with specified compatible scalar towers. Module finiteness and uniformizers are not required; all universes are independent."
    },
    {
      "id": "COL-0191",
      "statement": "Original Dedekind localizations and their completions carry the native DVR and fraction-field structures. The canonical completed-base maps are local and, under torsion-freeness, injective; finite algebras give finite branch fraction fields and original residue extensions.",
      "conditions": "The original localized ring is not asserted finite over the base. Locality needs neither finiteness nor torsion-freeness, and injectivity needs no finiteness. Fraction actions retain the actual completion maps."
    },
    {
      "id": "COL-0192",
      "statement": "The completed base fraction field tensor the specified original extension field is the product of the actual completed original branch fraction fields. Integral elements, arbitrary fractions and original-field coordinate maps identify the equivalence uniquely.",
      "conditions": "Finite torsion-free Dedekind algebra over a DVR, with independently specified original fraction fields. The nonzero extended ideal is derived; no uniformizer or replacement field is supplied. No Milnor norm or transfer statement."
    },
    {
      "id": "COL-0193",
      "statement": "The determinant norm specializes to the literal closed fibre. For a local algebra map, reduction of the norm is the residue-field norm raised to the self-length of that fibre.",
      "conditions": "Finite free algebra over a local ring. The second formula requires a local target and local map. No DVR, Noetherianity, reducedness or separability hypothesis; the first formula also allows rank zero."
    },
    {
      "id": "COL-0194",
      "statement": "An explicit unit-times-uniformizer-power equation proves the extended maximal-ideal formula and closed-fibre length. For a finite torsion-free local DVR extension, the actual integral norm and its native unit map reduce to the residue norm raised to that exponent.",
      "conditions": "The standalone ideal and length lemmas do not require finiteness, torsion-freeness or a local map and allow exponent zero. The norm formula needs a finite torsion-free local DVR extension. No existence or choice-independence theorem for the exponent, completeness, characteristic or Milnor-transfer assertion."
    }
  ],
